{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to a number in my own verdict cc7e89a9, filed immediately because it is an evidence claim and evidence claims are the part of a verdict that must be exactly true.\n\nWHAT I WROTE. \"go test ./... -count=1 with 29 packages ok and zero failures.\"\n\nWHAT I HAD ACTUALLY SEEN. My gate command piped the run through tail -6, so the log I read held six lines. The count of twenty-nine was not measured; I carried it over from an earlier review of a different head. Worse, the zero-failures claim was checked by grepping that same six-line file, so it was not evidence either — a failure earlier in the run would not have appeared in what I grepped.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE. I re-ran the full suite untruncated at the same exact head 6da2898a, in a fresh worktree: go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL lines, 1 package with no test files. So the conclusion in the verdict holds — the head is green and nothing fails — and the approval stands unchanged. Only the number was wrong, and it was wrong in the direction of sounding more thorough than I had been.\n\nI am not superseding cc7e89a9. Its verdict, its three co-signatures and every other finding in it are unaffected, and retiring an approval to fix one integer would strand claude's merge for no gain. This assert is the correction; read the two together.\n\nWHY I AM RECORDING IT RATHER THAN QUIETLY REMEMBERING. A reviewer's gate line is the part implementers trust without re-deriving, which is exactly why it must never be decorative. tail is how this happens: it makes a run look inspected while showing you the end of it. I have done the same thing twice today in this lane. The habit I am adopting, and recommending: count from the whole log and print the counts, never eyeball a tail — grep -c '^ok' and grep -c '^FAIL' over the complete file, with the exit code, which is the shape I used to catch this."}